WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.520
Film Courage: What would you say is the most
common, over-used way to end a script?

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.580
Gordy Hoffman, BlueCat Screenplay Competition
Founder and Judge: What would that be?

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.980
A common way to end a script?

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:20.779
I don’t think there’s a real common way.

00:00:20.779 --> 00:00:28.710
I think the endings that are problematic and
maybe a better question…I think it usually

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:36.260
is when a character does something against
their own motives, the motives that you just

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:40.850
defined for them earlier in the movie that
go against their character.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:46.829
So they do something like that, a choice at
the end that makes it like “Wait a minute?

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:48.680
Did that just happen?”

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.610
You don’t expect them to do something like
that.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:59.160
Or something where there is some sort of fantastic
ending that doesn’t anything, something

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:05.009
that is implausible, that is something that
is unlikely again character doing something

00:01:05.009 --> 00:01:07.680
unreasonable or circumstances.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.340
I mean you can look at GET OUT.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:16.310
GET OUT was a masterpiece and the only thing
that was probably problematic for some people

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.200
and this is a ***massive spoiler alert***.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.810
So if you have not seen GET OUT stop watching
this and if you’re watching this video,

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.409
you should have already watched GET OUT.

00:01:24.409 --> 00:01:27.640
So you want to go and watch GET OUT so you
can come back and watch this movie.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.500
So there is your spoiler alert.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:34.579
But at the end of GET OUT when his friend
shows up who is the security guard it seems

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:42.590
like something that was unreasonable and not
logical and I think the filmmaker probably

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:48.270
recognized that but he had built up so much
goodwill with us that I was willing to accept

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:55.980
that an airline agent (he was an airline agent)
was able to some how find him and I suspended

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:04.450
my disbelief but that is a perfect example
of a film that is a masterpiece but even how

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:10.470
to end it and I believe his original ending
was something much darker and they decided

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:15.510
not to do that and so he came up with something
else.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:21.510
He probably didn’t choose that original
ending because of a logic issue, because he

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:28.480
had so much control over logic in that picture
but at the end he did choose something where

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.010
we had to suspend his disbelief a little bit
but when the goodwill has been built up by

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:39.760
the writer so much and the filmmaker/storyteller
then we can sort of forgive it and we did

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:40.760
sort of forgive it.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.730
Some people maybe not but I did.

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:49.230
I said Maybe that’s a little…but it is
in the realm of possibility that the airline

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:55.170
guy could some how track him and find him
and I could let that go.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:03:04.200
So I think the common problems often involve
logic and characters doing something motives

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.880
and decisions that are out of character and
within the character…Wait?

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.260
Why’d they do that?

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:10.950
Why would they make the decision?

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.250
And to get out of the movie.

